      "name": "Error Alert",
      "kind": "custom",
      "refersTo": "alert",
      "description": "Explicit recovery feedback with an alert role.",
      "html": "<div class=\"ds-alert\" role=\"alert\">Connection lost. Refresh to read the saved game.</div>",
      "css": ".ds-alert{border:1px solid var(--error,#a23330);border-radius:var(--radius,.75rem);background:var(--muted-surface,#e9ecdf);color:var(--error,#a23330);padding:1rem 1.125rem;font-family:\"Readex Pro\",system-ui,sans-serif;font-size:.875rem;line-height:1.7}"
    }
  ],
  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Modern Game Club",
    "overview": "Warm paper and deep forest framing make the game welcoming; lime makes the next action easy to find. Blue X and coral O give the board its own visual language. Flat color, geometric marks and a locally hosted typeface carry the identity across the lobby, live game, forms, tables and legal pages.\n\nExpressive scale belongs to the introduction and the board. Supporting screens use the same controls with quieter type and clear reading order. Authored SVG supplies the game artwork and icons; motion describes confirmed state changes, then stops. The system uses no gradients, remote fonts or decorative loops.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Warm paper surfaces with forest framing and lime actions.",
      "One local type family for Latin and Arabic.",
      "Geometric X/O marks with distinct shape and color.",
      "Flat everyday surfaces and deliberate modal depth.",
      "Bounded motion, stable controls and explicit state feedback."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Shape Before Color Rule",
        "body": "Keep X/O geometry, text labels and active-state cues alongside color; color alone must never carry the game state.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Shared Script Rule",
        "body": "Use the same local family for English, French and Arabic; preserve translated labels and natural wrapping rather than forcing uppercase interface copy.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Logical Flow Rule",
        "body": "Use logical spacing and isolated player names for RTL; keep the physical game board and invitation codes LTR so positions remain consistent across languages.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Modal Depth Rule",
        "body": "Reserve floating elevation for modal interruption; use tonal surfaces and borders for normal content.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Confirmed Motion Rule",
        "body": "Animate committed state without delaying input, retrying uncertain moves, replaying initial marks or replacing focused cell buttons.",
        "section": "components"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** reuse shared semantic colors in both themes, keep lime actions legible with forest ink and preserve the body-text contrast requirement (4.5:1).",
      "**Do** preserve translated labels, logical layout, LTR board positions and isolated player names.",
      "**Do** retain native controls, visible focus, stable board buttons and explicit connection recovery.",
      "**Do** use short SVG/CSS state motion that finishes and respects reduced motion.",
      "**Do** verify rendered desktop, 360px and narrow 320px reflow, both themes, French and Arabic RTL when changing the interface."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** add gradients, remote font requests or decorative animation loops.",
      "**Don't** replace game labels or shape cues with color alone.",
      "**Don't** place ordinary content on floating shadows or turn every section into an enclosed card.",
      "**Don't** delay rematch navigation or invent optimistic board state for an animation.",
      "**Don't** infer completed visual or accessibility verification from this source-based design record."
    ]
  }
}
